# A mutating example: restart exactly one already-known systemd unit.
#
# `systemctl restart` only tells us that systemd accepted and completed the
# restart job. The yesh operation therefore requires a postcondition and
# reads the unit again before reporting success. The restart diagnostic also
# records Result, ExecMainCode, ExecMainStatus and the monotonic active-entry
# timestamp; those fields explain a failure but do not prove application
# readiness.
#
# `within 30s` is one finite client budget for both restart and verification.
# It is deliberately not `systemctl --wait`: --wait can wait for a
# long-lived service to terminate rather than confirm that it is active.
#
# The unit name is explicit. `systemd-resolved` without `.service`, paths,
# globs and shell operators are rejected; yesh must not silently broaden the
# target selected by the author.
#
# Bash equivalent (less safe to review):
#   systemctl restart systemd-resolved.service && \
#     test "$(systemctl show -p Id --value systemd-resolved.service)" = systemd-resolved.service && \
#     test "$(systemctl show -p ActiveState --value systemd-resolved.service)" = active
# The yesh form keeps argv boundaries, timeout and the postcondition visible.
# It does not add `sudo`, enable the unit, or repair a failed service.
service restart systemd-resolved.service until active within 30s
